  Too much level gain early can be a big problem.  In the game 
mentioned below Fluffy the kamikaze trap finder bit the dust 
early.  No pet means I kill all the monsters and my level goes up
too fast.   It also means there was no pet to help me when I did
encounter tough monsters.  

  Even with good equipment I had to turn back before Sokoban
because the monsters generated by my character level just got too
tough.  And wizards benefit a great deal early from the oracle 
(spellbooks) and sokoban (strength & food).

  I've noticed the problem before, but always died trying to get
to Sokoban anyway.  I'm happy with myself for finally taking
my own advice: "Run away!  Run away!"

>
>	They may have a point in the early game, but, conveniently, it
>stops being useful to keep your level down about the time you start
>running into wraiths.

   Don't forget, it's the levels you gain after your character is
level 30 that count:  You still get hit point & mana increases 
based on your Con and Wis, respectively.

  So chug blessed potions of gain ability and enlightenment until
Wis is maxed out.

   Then put on your best rings of gain constitution, and wear a 
+5 helm of brilliance before leveling up.

  Especially in this case where you know when you're about to 
encounter wraiths.

  Another good trick is to lead wraiths downstairs from the VotD
so they'll be more likely to leave a corpse.  They float right
over pit traps so you don't need to fill those in.

  Leading wraiths upstairs from the VotD has its good/bad points.
You can get more of them upstairs in one go.  But you then need
to lead them up to the level above the castle before they have 
an increased chance of leaving a corpse.  TDDTOE.  Again, they
float right over the trapdoors so it's a straight shot to the 
upstairs.

  Leading wraiths is a lot like leading a pet: be in line of 
sight and within 2-3 squares distance seems to work best.

  You can also do it on Orcus level:  lead the wraiths in the
graveyard downstairs for a better chance of them leaving a 
corpse.

  Same I think on the priest quest:  you have to lead them back
thru the portal on the quest home level.

  This is because the devteam gave wraiths a much reduced chance 
of leaving a corpse on graveyard levels, since a number of 
wraiths will be pre-generated there.  So the VotD, Orcus, the
Priest quest graveyard levels, and of course, graveyard levels
are designated graveyard levels.

  This is the same for the graveyard in Moloch's Sanctum.  But
there the clock is ticking and you just want to get through the
grave yard asap to stop the left hand side of the level from
filling up with monsters.  If indeed you don't try the "Here I am
again! trick."

  Even with the best protection a wraith will sometimes drain a 
level from your characters.  If you kill any monster (including
the wraith) before getting level-drained again it won't matter.

  For details (and there are many) on how hit points and mana
increase when a character levels up, see:

<http://www.spod-central.org/~psmith/nh/hppw-343.txt>

  Most of the details are outside of your control.


  This is an approach to powergaming where attention to detail 
really pays off, i.e. having Con & Wis be as high as possible 
when leveling up.  An early ring of gain constitution can also
help if you watch your experience points for when you're about
to level up.

  Since even with max Con & Wis the gains per level aren't great
you want to do it a lot, hence all the mucking about with 
wraiths.

  Bumping up charisma (b!oGA & magic trap dancing) & consorting
with foocubi is another way to gain a lot of levels.  That 
doesn't apply here because you can't reverse-genocide foocubi. 
('That shall not be done.')

  My favorite way of getting foocubi is to be human and chaotic
and sacrifice a lot of humans.  The Castle & Fort Ludious are
prime sources of human soldiers.  You need an altar within 
reasonable distance of them.  (I've got a game with 20 foocubi
generated from Fort Ludious soldiers waiting.  And I'm not 
finished with the fort yet.  Now all I need is an amulet of 
change because 75% of the foocubi are the wrong sex.)

  The soldiers will also thoughtfully bring all their equipment
along for you to buc id on the altar & check for enchantment.
(In the game mentioned above I'm wearing +3 gloves & +4 low boots
from Ft. Ludious soldiers.  The +5 gloves I'm keeping safe in a
chest until I can fooproof them with a cursed scroll of destroy 
armor.)

  Reasonable is up to you to define.  How close together the 
up/down stairs are will come into it.  How much are 300 max hit 
points and 500 max mana points, before alchemy, worth to you?



